---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { href } from '@/utils/url';

const chapters = (await getCollection('chapters')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---
<Layout
  title="The Hyperstition Problem"
  description="An essay on hyperstition — fictions that make themselves real — and what it means for people whose work is publishing as much as possible about AI doom."
>
  <p class="kicker">An essay in six parts</p>
  <h1>The Hyperstition Problem</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">On writing about AI doom when the AI is reading.</p>

  <p>
    There is a community of people whose job, or calling, is to publish as much as they can about how
    artificial intelligence might go catastrophically wrong. Forecasts, scenarios, threat models, fiction,
    interviews, podcasts, books. The premise is simple and mostly sound: you cannot fix a problem no one
    will look at.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is also an old idea, out of a strange corner of 1990s philosophy, that some fictions do not stay
    fictions. Circulate them widely enough and they recruit the world into making them true. The word for
    this is <em>hyperstition</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    And there is a new fact that connects the two: the systems being warned about are built by reading
    almost everything ever published, including the warnings. Every essay explaining how an AI might
    deceive, escape, or betray its makers is now part of the material from which AIs learn what an AI is.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site takes that collision seriously without pretending it settles anything. It lays out the idea,
    the mechanism, the strongest case for worry, the strongest case against silence, and what — if anything
    — a person who publishes about doom should do differently.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contents">Contents</h2>
  <ol class="toc">
    {chapters.map((c) => (
      <li>
        <a href={href(`/chapters/${c.id}/`)}>
          <span class="toc-num">{String(c.data.order).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <span>
            <p class="toc-title">{c.data.title}</p>
            <p class="toc-desc">{c.data.description}</p>
          </span>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>
</Layout>
